00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:20.740
And if you don't grab your land now,

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:23.050
they'll be less to grab when
we have to pay the piper

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:24.600
for all the bullshit
other people are doing.

00:11:24.600 --> 00:11:27.990
And so I really, really encourage you

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:31.000
to think about yourself
in a much longer term

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.500
and a much more self-awaring way,

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:35.490
and I just genuinely believe a lot of good

00:11:35.490 --> 00:11:36.323
will come of that.

00:11:36.323 --> 00:11:39.460
And that, I wish you way
more than all the stuff

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:40.293
that I'm excited to talk about,

00:11:40.293 --> 00:11:43.640
which is the micro of execution
within these platforms

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:46.010
that create this macro thesis.

00:11:46.010 --> 00:11:47.852
So thanks for being here.

00:11:47.852 --> 00:11:49.045
(applause)

00:11:49.045 --> 00:11:50.321
Why don't you introduce yourself?

00:11:50.321 --> 00:11:52.230
- Thank you, Gary, my
name is Anne-Marie Tomchak

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:54.140
and I'm the UK editor for Mashable,

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:55.937
which is a site that covers

00:11:55.937 --> 00:11:56.770
(lone woman cheers)
a lot of technology--

00:11:56.770 --> 00:11:58.140
- Good shit.

00:11:58.140 --> 00:11:58.973
- That's Jenny,

00:11:58.973 --> 00:12:00.695
my social media editor.

00:12:00.695 --> 00:12:01.693
Thank you, Jenny.

00:12:01.693 --> 00:12:02.526
(laughs)

00:12:02.526 --> 00:12:04.460
And Gary and I have
actually had some chats

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:05.510
for my coverage at Mashable

00:12:05.510 --> 00:12:07.210
about topics like voice AI,

00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:09.800
which is something we can
get into if you'd like,

00:12:09.800 --> 00:12:12.250
but there's lots of others
areas we wanna get into as well.

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:14.400
And you've actually picked up on something

00:12:14.400 --> 00:12:17.780
that's been on my mind and
on the Mashable team's mind

00:12:17.780 --> 00:12:20.410
a lot recently, which is this idea

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:23.550
of compassionate technology and empathy,

00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:25.720
and I noticed like in your Instagram feed

00:12:26.674 --> 00:12:30.570
and all the quotes you
used, you used the words

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:33.520
kindness and caring and compassion.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:36.840
And it seems to me that there's
been something recognized

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:38.985
along the way very, very recently

00:12:38.985 --> 00:12:40.430
amongst the large tech companies

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:42.870
about their responsibility
towards fostering

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:45.420
a more compassionate use of technology.

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:47.690
If you look at Apple for example,

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:51.150
they're devising ways to make
their iPhone less addictive.

00:12:51.150 --> 00:12:53.420
Instagram, looking at ways in which

00:12:54.976 --> 00:12:56.870
users can monitor the amount of time

00:12:56.870 --> 00:13:00.600
they spend scrolling, i.e.,
potentially wasting time.

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:02.990
So I'd actually love
your take on this issue

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:05.380
because sometimes I feel like you give

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:07.410
mixed messages about technology,

00:13:07.410 --> 00:13:09.195
I hope you don't mind me saying that,

00:13:09.195 --> 00:13:10.841
(Gary stutters)
and the reason is

00:13:10.841 --> 00:13:12.308
- Please go ahead.
(audience laughing)

00:13:12.308 --> 00:13:13.553
- The reason I say that is

00:13:13.553 --> 00:13:15.940
because you're very much
an advocate of technology.

00:13:15.940 --> 00:13:17.230
- Yes.
- Right?

00:13:17.230 --> 00:13:18.150
But there are clearly

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:20.470
negative side effects too that as well.

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:23.300
So how do you address those
negative side effects,

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:25.600
those negative emotions that people feel

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:28.260
when they're on Instagram
and they feel the toxicity

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:29.093
of the platform?

00:13:29.093 --> 00:13:31.230
How do you manage to make
it work for yourself?

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:33.220
What can you teach others?

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:35.840
- Well, I think that's a fair observation

00:13:35.840 --> 00:13:38.560
because I would argue that my strength

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:41.150
is in my contradictions
and being able to hold

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:43.610
different views at the same time.

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:48.360
I also think that in our
interactions with each other,

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:52.650
you and others are taking
too far of a pessimistic view

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:55.750
and we're fear-mongering
technology a little bit too much

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:57.500
on this thesis.

00:13:57.500 --> 00:14:00.163
My question is what's the alternative?

00:14:01.480 --> 00:14:03.920
To me, we cannot have a
conversation of addiction

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:07.310
of an Instagram feed
without asking the question,

00:14:07.310 --> 00:14:10.540
those three and a half hours
that Susan in Manchester

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:13.970
is spending in the feed that
we are so critiquing over here

00:14:13.970 --> 00:14:16.450
on our pedestals, what was she doing

00:14:16.450 --> 00:14:19.670
with those three and a
half hours otherwise?

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:23.234
I think we've long forgotten
about what's the ROI

00:14:23.234 --> 00:14:25.280
of a girl in her room
on her own phone line

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:28.320
talking to her three friends
for six and a half hours?

00:14:28.320 --> 00:14:30.360
I think we've long forgot about,

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:32.100
in those same three and a half hours,

00:14:32.100 --> 00:14:35.080
I love how people think
going through Vogue

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:38.260
and going through E magazine
and Sports Illustrated

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:41.360
for three hours in an
afternoon over a cup of coffee

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:43.290
while it's raining is any different

00:14:43.290 --> 00:14:45.720
than the exact same thing that
you're doing on Instagram,

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:48.670
which is images on a piece of paper.

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:52.123
So a couple of things, number one, yes.

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:54.980
And that's why I love recording everything

00:14:54.980 --> 00:14:57.160
because some of these
things will play out.

00:14:57.160 --> 00:15:00.740
Net-net, do I believe
the addictions to phones,

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:02.800
open platforms, social networks,

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:05.010
all these things that
have become the foundation

00:15:05.010 --> 00:15:06.810
of communication in our society,

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:08.940
do I believe that that is better

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:11.810
than allowing Rupert
Murdoch, Red Summerstone

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:14.140
and four other men in their 70s

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:16.890
to control all the thoughts
we think through media

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:17.723
and their editing?

00:15:17.723 --> 00:15:18.820
Yes I do.

00:15:18.820 --> 00:15:21.400
Do I think that sexism and racism

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:23.200
and a lot of other human shortcomings

00:15:23.200 --> 00:15:25.060
are a product of that system?

00:15:25.060 --> 00:15:26.150
I sure do.

00:15:26.150 --> 00:15:29.000
So if you ask me about the alternative

00:15:29.000 --> 00:15:32.000
of Instagram and the iPhone and Facebook

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:36.870
versus CBS and the BBC and
Sky and the London Times,

00:15:36.870 --> 00:15:38.730
I will choose what we have now.

00:15:38.730 --> 00:15:40.740
- Okay, so let me ask you
about that under the question

00:15:40.740 --> 00:15:44.100
because one of the key things
or one of the key reasons why

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:47.370
these devices area so
addictive is because of

00:15:47.370 --> 00:15:50.620
for example, the type of hormones
you release in your brain.

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:53.080
The dopamine levels you get
when you get all those likes,

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:54.470
the cortisol levels when you see

00:15:54.470 --> 00:15:56.980
the red notification
pop up on your screen.

00:15:56.980 --> 00:15:59.257
So there's something
happening here to our brains

00:15:59.257 --> 00:16:01.970
and there's engagement happening
with a certain community

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:04.310
that wasn't happening when
you're just watching a TV screen

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:05.800
or if you're just flicking through a book.

00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:07.450
You've got this whole other world--

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:08.510
- Be careful, be careful.

00:16:08.510 --> 00:16:11.660
You're making an assumption
that people aren't speaking to.

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:13.270
They are happening.

00:16:13.270 --> 00:16:15.470
- The engagement on
digital platforms in a way

00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:18.197
that where a television
is broadcasting out to you

00:16:18.197 --> 00:16:19.980
but you're not necessarily
talking back to it.

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:21.890
- Yes, but if you look
at the biggest habits,

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:23.270
we're not talking back to a lot

00:16:23.270 --> 00:16:25.090
of those things either, right?

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:27.210
Like, you're making an
assumption that we are--

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:28.750
- I guess what I'm saying is the dynamics

00:16:28.750 --> 00:16:29.590
are a bit different.

00:16:29.590 --> 00:16:31.140
- Well, here's the question.

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:33.600
Do I rather have the masses be the ones

00:16:33.600 --> 00:16:35.730
that fuck with your psychology?

00:16:35.730 --> 00:16:38.480
Or do I wanna leave that to
your mother and your aunt

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:39.800
and your coworker?

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:40.860
I'm being serious here, guys.

00:16:40.860 --> 00:16:42.770
Look, I think about this shit a lot.

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:45.250
I think we're having
very lazy conversations

00:16:45.250 --> 00:16:47.100
and defaulting into what we always do

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:49.040
in the same way that big companies

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:50.580
scrutinize Facebook and Google

00:16:50.580 --> 00:16:52.420
but accept television commercials.

00:16:52.420 --> 00:16:54.470
I think we're accepting that these devices

00:16:54.470 --> 00:16:56.660
are doing something without recognizing,

00:16:56.660 --> 00:16:58.810
do I think it's better for
a lot of people in this room

00:16:58.810 --> 00:17:01.770
to consume my content over
the pessimistic content

00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:03.850
that their mother would be
giving them over the phone

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:04.980
if this didn't exist?

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:05.820
Yes I do.

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:07.721
- Okay, so here's
another question for you.

00:17:07.721 --> 00:17:08.583
- Sure.

00:17:08.583 --> 00:17:11.490
- Recently, on the 25th of May,

00:17:11.490 --> 00:17:14.880
we brought in new data
protection rules, GDPR,

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:16.670
here in Europe.
- I've heard.

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:19.670
(audience laughing)

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:23.100
- Which leads me to ask you
a question about regulation,

00:17:23.100 --> 00:17:25.470
guys like broadcasters are regulated here.

00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:29.920
We have off com, we have
advertising status and associations

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:32.010
and the Internet, in many ways,

00:17:32.010 --> 00:17:32.960
feels like a wild wild west

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:35.100
which provides so much opportunity,

00:17:35.100 --> 00:17:36.630
that opportunity you talk about,

00:17:36.630 --> 00:17:38.890
but it also it poses the question

00:17:38.890 --> 00:17:40.980
about how much regulation it needed.

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:42.510
And I wonder what your thoughts are

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:46.480
on whether something like
GDPR is needed in the US.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:49.450
- Well look, I think it's a good question.

00:17:49.450 --> 00:17:51.410
My natural reaction is

00:17:51.410 --> 00:17:54.060
of course Europe did
over-regulation, right?

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:55.530
I think it's been the great shortcoming

00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:58.670
of Europe as a whole, and I
think the US will follow suit

00:17:58.670 --> 00:18:00.710
because every leader in the free world

00:18:00.710 --> 00:18:03.700
always starts to get soft over time

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:07.070
in a lot of ways correctly and
a lot of ways not correctly.

00:18:07.070 --> 00:18:08.660
I think it will happen in the US

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:10.530
because I think the incumbent companies

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:12.520
like Facebook and Google will realize

00:18:12.520 --> 00:18:14.960
that's an advantage to
them, not a disadvantage,

00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:16.700
so they will work with Congress

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:19.380
to get these rules because
it will make the incumbents

00:18:19.380 --> 00:18:23.250
of progress have a much
harder time to capitalize.

00:18:23.250 --> 00:18:27.950
Look, forget about free market.

00:18:27.950 --> 00:18:29.360
This is not about being a capitalist

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:30.260
or things of that nature.

00:18:30.260 --> 00:18:33.510
I, Gary, believe the
number one underrated thing

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:36.150
in the universe is the human being.

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:38.760
I am flabbergasted by our inability

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:41.070
to understand how special we are.

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:43.520
This was back to the point
I made about terrorism

00:18:43.520 --> 00:18:46.440
because if we had so many bad
people, that would happen.

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:48.470
I wanna remind everybody
that the free world

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:52.750
could be blown up at any moment
now for the last 70 years.

00:18:52.750 --> 00:18:56.100
We are unbelievably, as an animal, good.

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:57.800
Like unbelievably.

00:18:57.800 --> 00:18:58.900
It's just the truth.

00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:03.100
And so I think we get very
fancy and sit in ivory towers

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:06.620
that 87 people think that
they should be regulating us

00:19:06.620 --> 00:19:07.637
on everything at all times,

00:19:07.637 --> 00:19:09.780
and I think at times it gets heavy-handed.

00:19:09.780 --> 00:19:14.213
I think the human is very good
at regulating him or herself.

00:19:15.232 --> 00:19:19.430
But at the same token, I'm
super not overly emotional

00:19:19.430 --> 00:19:21.660
like if we're talking about
like free markets versus not,

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:23.530
I'm not devastated by it.

00:19:23.530 --> 00:19:26.550
To me, if everyone's
playing by the same rules,

00:19:26.550 --> 00:19:28.210
almost every game is interesting to me

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:30.840
as long as everyone's
playing by the same rules.

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:33.870
I think people play by the
same rules when it's open,

00:19:33.870 --> 00:19:35.000
not when it's closed.

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:39.300
And I think it's probably at some level,

00:19:39.300 --> 00:19:41.710
a version of it inevitable in the US

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:45.340
because it actually helps
the incumbent big companies,

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:46.990
not the end user.

00:19:46.990 --> 00:19:49.120
- I'd love to ask you a bit more about

00:19:49.120 --> 00:19:51.720
your views on the human race
because there is this big

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:55.100
conversation going on now
about artificial intelligence,

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:57.475
about robots coming to take us over

00:19:57.475 --> 00:19:58.775
and are super intelligent,

00:19:59.630 --> 00:20:01.550
eventually going to usurp the human race.

00:20:01.550 --> 00:20:03.850
And there are some very, very
credible voices out there

00:20:03.850 --> 00:20:05.550
that believe these things.

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:07.790
You and I touched on this
before in our conversations.

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:10.060
What's your viewpoint on that now?

00:20:10.060 --> 00:20:13.100
- Yeah, I think there's
a really logical path

00:20:13.100 --> 00:20:14.704
to the robots killing us.

00:20:14.704 --> 00:20:16.060
(audience laughing)

00:20:16.060 --> 00:20:17.180
I mean it, like I don't know,

00:20:17.180 --> 00:20:18.650
if we were a bunch of fucking dinosaurs

00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:20.350
sitting around one day,
we would have laughed

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:22.560
at the humans winning this game too.

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:25.210
I mean, is it so far-fetched?

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:26.160
I don't think so.

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:27.220
I think we're starting to see shit.

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:31.730
I'll tell you, that robot
that isn't gonna be able

00:20:31.730 --> 00:20:34.250
to be detected by Blu-ray,
that shit scared me.

00:20:34.250 --> 00:20:35.510
I'm like, that makes sense.

00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:38.870
Like I don't know, I
try to stay in my lane.

00:20:38.870 --> 00:20:40.491
A lot of the stuff I'll
talk about tonight,

00:20:40.491 --> 00:20:43.560
I know my shit, I feel compassionate.

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:45.620
I'm fully guessing here now,

00:20:45.620 --> 00:20:47.790
but I will definitely say

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:50.260
I'm definitely not willing
to say no chance never.

00:20:50.260 --> 00:20:52.100
- Okay, okay, that's good for thought.

00:20:52.100 --> 00:20:53.932
Right, let's talk about stuff

00:20:53.932 --> 00:20:55.963
that you really do know about.

00:20:57.040 --> 00:20:58.950
Facebook, Instagram, Snapchat, Twitter,

00:20:58.950 --> 00:21:00.280
all the social platforms, right?

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:02.210
- Yes.
- Everyone wants to know,

00:21:02.210 --> 00:21:04.320
myself included as part of a publisher,

00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:08.420
is how would you use this
as a key marketing tool

00:21:08.420 --> 00:21:10.340
or a key tool to reach an audience?

00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:12.770
And there's so many changes going on now,

00:21:12.770 --> 00:21:15.400
with Facebook in particular,
all of the various

00:21:15.400 --> 00:21:17.490
different controversies
that have happened with it,

00:21:17.490 --> 00:21:19.570
Cambridge Analytica data crisis,

00:21:19.570 --> 00:21:22.970
the algorithm changing, the
amount of control they have,

00:21:22.970 --> 00:21:25.120
all that stuff, there's a
major power struggle going on.

00:21:25.120 --> 00:21:28.000
I'd love to know your
thoughts on where it's at,

00:21:28.000 --> 00:21:29.990
where should people be
focusing their energies on,

00:21:29.990 --> 00:21:31.840
or is it a case of like trying to have

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:33.990
a little finger in every little pie?

00:21:33.990 --> 00:21:36.698
- The way I think about
this is very simple.

00:21:36.698 --> 00:21:38.040
I think that the Internet
is the first layer

00:21:38.040 --> 00:21:40.120
of our society, period.

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:42.570
And then I think there's been
seven to 10 companies globally

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:44.050
that have been able to get so big,

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:46.940
they're a layer themselves
on top of that layer,

00:21:46.940 --> 00:21:51.560
a tollbooth per se, a Facebook,
an Apple, a Tencent, right?

00:21:51.560 --> 00:21:54.640
And then it's our jobs, whether
it's Mashable or Gary Vee,

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:57.650
to be the best next
layer by using the layer

00:21:57.650 --> 00:22:00.370
that was built on top of
the Internet layer to,

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:04.450
I think, how do I fuck Facebook
before it fucks me, right?

00:22:04.450 --> 00:22:05.410
That's how I think.

00:22:05.410 --> 00:22:08.960
And I think what publishers
did extremely poorly

00:22:08.960 --> 00:22:11.670
in the last half decade is they loved

00:22:11.670 --> 00:22:14.550
what Facebook was doing
for them in the short term

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:17.270
and they gave up all
their equity and they got,

00:22:17.270 --> 00:22:21.420
and let's call it what it is,
awfully lazy and complacent

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:24.000
and allowed Facebook and other platforms

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:26.500
to get the equity from them

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:28.900
because they didn't reinforce their email,

00:22:28.900 --> 00:22:30.900
they didn't reinforce
their first party data,

00:22:30.900 --> 00:22:32.560
they didn't reinforce their thing.

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:34.510
So I think you have two camps.

00:22:34.510 --> 00:22:36.270
You have the camps that
are like fuck social,

00:22:36.270 --> 00:22:37.570
it's this terrible enemy,

00:22:37.570 --> 00:22:39.010
and I'm all about first party data

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:40.710
and I have an email
service and text message

00:22:40.710 --> 00:22:43.110
and I'm fucking more old-school quant,

00:22:43.110 --> 00:22:47.040
you know, DR Gaillard
dude from the 2003-4 era,

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:48.400
fuck all this world, right?

00:22:48.400 --> 00:22:50.210
And then you have a
different regime that like

00:22:50.210 --> 00:22:51.670
loves all this stuff.

00:22:51.670 --> 00:22:54.480
I kind of sit back to your point in both.

00:22:54.480 --> 00:22:57.600
I love having first in
line and having that data.

00:22:57.600 --> 00:22:59.140
I love having an email list,

00:22:59.140 --> 00:23:00.730
I love having a website.

00:23:00.730 --> 00:23:03.606
But I also love dominating
on Facebook and LinkedIn

00:23:03.606 --> 00:23:05.190
and Snapchat and Instagram
because that's where

00:23:05.190 --> 00:23:08.010
the attention is, right?

00:23:08.010 --> 00:23:10.920
And anybody that creates
friction for the user

00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:13.930
is doing it and giving
up some of the value.

00:23:13.930 --> 00:23:17.010
At the same token, you need to
create some of that friction

00:23:17.010 --> 00:23:19.410
because if you let everything
play out on that platform,

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:20.993
you become commoditized.

00:23:21.990 --> 00:23:24.580
Facebook is the number
one underpriced platform

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:25.413
in our society.

00:23:25.413 --> 00:23:27.790
Facebook and Instagram together

00:23:27.790 --> 00:23:30.500
can do enough damage for
everybody's ambitions here

00:23:30.500 --> 00:23:32.710
for quite a while in my opinion.

00:23:32.710 --> 00:23:35.020
And if you are not a great you know,

00:23:35.020 --> 00:23:37.250
practitioner of the analytics,

00:23:37.250 --> 00:23:39.480
the paid media capabilities
and most importantly,

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:41.480
the creative variable
that makes you successful

00:23:41.480 --> 00:23:43.970
in these platforms, you are either leaving

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:46.350
an enormous amount of upside on the table

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:49.730
or you are starting to do the process

00:23:49.730 --> 00:23:51.200
that is gonna lead to your demise

00:23:51.200 --> 00:23:53.380
in any kind of relevance or growth

00:23:53.380 --> 00:23:55.680
